  "explanationHtml": "<h3>What kind of puzzle this is</h3><p>Nothing here is translated for you except the phrase in the first sentence. What you do have is structure: many Nizaa entries share pieces (<span class=\"data\">nim</span> appears seven times, <span class=\"data\">nìì</span> three times), and many English glosses share ideas (\"puppy\" and \"little finger\" are both \"a small something\"; \"carpenter\", \"hunter\" and \"hairdresser\" are all \"a person who works with something\"). Solving the problem means lining up the repeated Nizaa pieces with the repeated English ideas.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 1: find the busiest word</h3><p>Seven entries contain <span class=\"data\">nim</span>, and <span class=\"data\">nim</span> also stands alone (entry 16). Which English idea shows up in seven glosses? Water does: \"water\" itself, \"cup (for drinking water)\", \"source of water\", plus things you can describe as water-related — soup (watery sauce), a duck (a water bird), a teardrop (eye water) and <em>Anthocleista vogelii</em>, which the notes tell you is a rainforest tree, i.e. a tree of wet places. So <span class=\"data\">nim</span> = water, and those seven entries are exactly entries 2, 9, 11, 16, 17, 20 and 21.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: fix the word order</h3><p>Compare entry 2, <span class=\"data\">ɓeè nim</span>, with entry 17, <span class=\"data\">nim ɓʉʉ</span>. One should be a \"water container\" and the other a \"water head\" — and \"source of water\" is literally the head (top, beginning) of a body of water. Since <span class=\"data\">ɓʉʉ</span> also appears alone as entry 5, the pattern is: the main noun (the \"head\" of the phrase, in the grammatical sense) comes <strong>first</strong>, and the word that modifies it comes second. So <span class=\"data\">ɓeè nim</span> is \"calabash of water\" = a cup, which forces entry 1, <span class=\"data\">ɓeè</span>, to be \"calabash\". The intro phrase agrees: <span class=\"data\">sìì jaŋgidḕ</span> = \"school\" is \"building for reading\".</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: chase the chains</h3><ul><li><span class=\"data\">ɓʉʉ</span> = head (5). Then <span class=\"data\">sìì ɓʉʉ</span> (18) = \"head of the hut\" = roof, so <span class=\"data\">sìì</span> = hut — the same <span class=\"data\">sìì</span> as in \"school\".</li><li>Two entries end in <span class=\"data\">-ŋwiiŋ</span> (4, 7), and two English glosses mean \"small X\": puppy and little finger. <span class=\"data\">ɓòw-ŋwiiŋ</span> must be puppy, so <span class=\"data\">ɓòw</span> alone (3) = dog; <span class=\"data\">cam-ŋwiiŋ</span> = little finger, so <span class=\"data\">cam</span> = finger/hand.</li><li>Two entries contain <span class=\"data\">fwɛ̀ɛŋ</span> (6, 8), and two glosses mean \"outer covering\": fingernail and tree bark. Since <span class=\"data\">cam</span> is already \"finger\", <span class=\"data\">cam fwɛ̀ɛŋ</span> = fingernail and <span class=\"data\">cún fwɛ̀ɛŋ</span> = tree bark, giving <span class=\"data\">cún</span> = tree. That makes <span class=\"data\">cún nim</span> (9) the \"water tree\", <em>Anthocleista vogelii</em>.</li><li><span class=\"data\">ɗɔ́ɔŋ</span> stands alone (10) and appears with <span class=\"data\">nim</span> (11): sauce and \"water sauce\" = soup. Entry 12, <span class=\"data\">hodḕ ɗɔ́ɔŋ</span>, is then \"spoon for sauce\" = sauce spoon (head first: <span class=\"data\">hodḕ</span> = spoon).</li><li>Three entries start with <span class=\"data\">nìì</span> (13, 14, 15) and three glosses are occupations. <span class=\"data\">nìì cún</span> = \"person of tree\" = carpenter. <span class=\"data\">nìì gírcí ɓʉʉ</span> contains \"head\", so it is the person who does something to heads = hairdresser, and <span class=\"data\">gírcí</span> = to shave. That leaves <span class=\"data\">nìì kwɛ́ɛŋ</span> = hunter, so <span class=\"data\">kwɛ́ɛŋ</span> means bush (wild country).</li><li>Finally <span class=\"data\">sìŋw kwɛ́ɛŋ</span> (19) = \"bird of the bush\" = wild bird and <span class=\"data\">sìŋw nim</span> (20) = \"bird of the water\" = duck, so <span class=\"data\">sìŋw</span> = bird. The last one left, <span class=\"data\">yír nim</span> (21), is teardrop, \"eye water\", with <span class=\"data\">yír</span> = eye.</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Step 4: the F2 translations</h3><p>Every answer fell out of the chains above: bush = <span class=\"data\">kwɛ́ɛŋ</span> (from hunter), hut = <span class=\"data\">sìì</span> (from roof and from \"school\"), to read = <span class=\"data\">jaŋgidḕ</span> (the second half of <span class=\"data\">sìì jaŋgidḕ</span>), to shave = <span class=\"data\">gírcí</span> (the middle of \"hairdresser\").</p>\n\n<h3>The big idea</h3><p>Languages build new vocabulary out of old vocabulary. English does it too (\"eyeball\", \"waterfowl\", \"woodworker\"), but Nizaa does it transparently and consistently, with the main noun first and its modifier second. Once you spot one such compound, it unlocks two words at a time, and the whole list collapses like a crossword.</p>",
